# Table

Use a table when shared columns help people compare values and understand relationships.

These are civica.uk reference implementations for component authors. They do not replace native Home Assistant behaviour or demonstrate a connection to a live home.

## When to use it

Use it for readings from several rooms or the values behind a chart. Give the table a caption and show units in the headings or cells. Right-align comparable numbers.

## When not to use it

Do not use a table for page layout or force a large data set into a tiny card. A summary list is clearer for the properties of one device; a short explanation may be clearer than a table.

## Home Assistant adaptation

Keep recorded zeros distinct from missing values. The Energy companion’s View data table is an implemented example with pagination and native period semantics; these broader table variants are reference patterns. Preserve the selected date, unit and timezone when presenting chart values.

## Accessibility and behaviour

Use proper row and column headers. A wide table may scroll inside a labelled, keyboard-focusable region while the rest of the page reflows. Preserve table semantics if you develop a stacked layout. Sorting needs real buttons, aria-sort and feedback, not a decorative arrow.

